\card{Remark}{ }{
\label{mk:AlgebraLiterature}

\p{For a commutative ring #{R}, the
\vocabk{algebra}{ca-0016} structure is equivalently an
\vocabk{#{R}-module}{ca-000X} structure on an associative ring #{A} with
identity, with the same addition, such that
##{
r \bu (x * y) = (r \bu x) * y = x * (r \bu y)
\qquad (r \in R,\ x,y \in A).
}
This is the formulation of \citet{Definition 1.6, p. 4}{jadczyk2019notes}.
Both formulations require associativity and an identity.}

\proof{
\p{Given the central ring homomorphism #{\rfun}, define
#{r \bu x=\rfun(r)*x}. The ring laws and the preservation of addition,
multiplication and identity by #{\rfun} give
##{
\begin{aligned}
r \bu (x+y)&=\rfun(r)*x+\rfun(r)*y,\\
(r+s)\bu x&=\rfun(r)*x+\rfun(s)*x,\\
r\bu(s\bu x)&=(\rfun(r)*\rfun(s))*x=(r*s)\bu x,\\
1_R\bu x&=1_A*x=x.
\end{aligned}
}
Thus the action defines an #{R}-module. Associativity gives
#{r\bu(x*y)=(r\bu x)*y}; centrality of #{\rfun(r)} then gives
#{(r\bu x)*y=x*(r\bu y)}.}

\p{Conversely, given the module formulation, put
#{\rfun(r)=r\bu 1_A}. The module axioms give
#{\rfun(r+s)=\rfun(r)+\rfun(s)} and #{\rfun(1_R)=1_A}.
They also give #{\rfun(0_R)=0_A}, by cancellation in
#{0_R\bu 1_A=(0_R\bu 1_A)+(0_R\bu 1_A)}.
Compatibility with multiplication implies
##{
\begin{aligned}
\rfun(r)*\rfun(s)
&=(r\bu 1_A)*(s\bu 1_A)\\
&=r\bu(1_A*(s\bu 1_A))\\
&=(r*s)\bu 1_A=\rfun(r*s),\\
\rfun(r)*x&=r\bu x=x*\rfun(r).
\end{aligned}
}
Hence #{\rfun} is a central, identity-preserving ring homomorphism,
and its induced action is the original one.
Starting with a central ring homomorphism instead recovers it because
#{\rfun(r)*1_A=\rfun(r)}.}
}

\p{The corresponding Mathlib constructions are
\leanref{Algebra.toModule} and \leanref{Algebra.ofModule}.
Mathlib also permits semirings; under the ring hypotheses here, the two
formulations describe the same structures.}
}
